# Shortest Unsorted Continuous Subarray — LeetCode 581

**Pattern:** Array + Two Pointers  
**Approach:** Find boundaries, then expand using minimum and maximum.

## Approach
1. Find `low`: the first index where `nums[i] > nums[i + 1]`.
2. Find `high`: the last index where `nums[i - 1] > nums[i]`.
3. If `low >= high`, the array is already sorted. Return `0`.
4. Find the minimum and maximum elements within `nums[low...high]`.
5. Expand `low` left while the previous element is greater than `min`.
6. Expand `high` right while the next element is smaller than `max`.
7. Return `high - low + 1`.

## Java Code

```java
class Solution {
    public int findUnsortedSubarray(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int low = n - 1;
        int high = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            if (nums[i] > nums[i + 1]) {
                low = Math.min(low, i);
            }
        }

        for (int i = n - 1; i > 0; i--) {
            if (nums[i - 1] > nums[i]) {
                high = Math.max(high, i);
            }
        }

        if (low >= high) return 0;

        int min = nums[low];
        int max = nums[low];

        for (int i = low; i <= high; i++) {
            min = Math.min(min, nums[i]);
            max = Math.max(max, nums[i]);
        }

        while (low > 0 && nums[low - 1] > min) {
            low--;
        }

        while (high < n - 1 && nums[high + 1] < max) {
            high++;
        }

        return high - low + 1;
    }
}
